Headache worse in morning, New-onset adult seizures, Focal neurological deficit

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-004
Reason for Visit: Unrelenting severe morning headache and witnessed first-time seizure.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a first-time adult seizure associated with progressive morning headaches and nausea. Immediate assessment is required to evaluate for elevated intracranial pressure and prevent catastrophic neurological decompensation prior to regular clinical hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My head has been pounding every morning, and my wife says I passed out and shook all over for two minutes today."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a progressive, persistent headache over the past month that is dull, unremitting, and explicitly worse in the morning upon awakening. The headache is directly exacerbated by maneuvers that increase intracranial pressure, such as coughing and bending over. The patient has been experiencing unexplained nausea and explosive vomiting without preceding dietary triggers. Today, the spouse witnessed a new-onset generalized seizure lasting two minutes, with post-ictal confusion. The spouse also notes recent subtle cognitive decline and rapidly progressive apathy. There is a progressive unsteadiness and gait disturbance reported over the last week.
Functional Status: Requiring assistance for mobilization due to severe ataxia and lethargy.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of recurrent seizures and falls.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 160/95 mmHg | HR: 58 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 12 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Somnolent but rousable, appearing chronically unwell, holding head.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals progressive, anatomically defined focal neurological deficits including a mild left-sided hemiparesis (power 4/5) and a left homonymous hemianopia. Fundoscopic examination reveals bilateral papilledema signifying severely elevated intracranial pressure. Gait is unsteady and broad-based. Diagnostic tetrad features observed include headache worse in the morning, new-onset adult seizure, and clear focal neurological deficits. Bradycardia and mild hypertension suggest early Cushing's reflex.
Environmental Observations: Darkened room, vomit bucket beside the bed.
Medication Reconciliation: Using over-the-counter paracetamol and ibuprofen with no relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Suspected Intracranial Space-Occupying Lesion - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Tension-Type Headache - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of papilledema, seizures, focal neurological deficits, and morning exacerbation with vomiting.

Meningitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Afebrile, absence of nuchal rigidity or photophobia.

Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Constant ataxia unrelated to head position changes, presence of upper motor neuron signs and seizures.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an ominous constellation of signs: progressive morning headaches, a first-ever adult seizure, papilledema, and focal neurological deficits. The vitals demonstrate a relative bradycardia and hypertension, indicating critically elevated intracranial pressure and impending brain herniation. Delaying assessment until morning would inevitably risk fatal mass effect or status epilepticus. Urgent after-hours deployment was mandated to identify these catastrophic neurological signs, stabilize the patient on site, and facilitate immediate, prioritized transfer via emergency medical services for urgent neuroimaging and neurosurgical intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate dispatch of emergency ambulance for ED transfer. Placed patient in a 30-degree head-up position to optimize cerebral venous drainage. Handover prepared for receiving emergency physician.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Step 1: By resting quietly with the head of the bed elevated, the patient actively reduces pressure in the skull, minimizing headache severity.
Step 2: By avoiding straining or sudden head movements, the patient prevents dangerous spikes in brain pressure, avoiding further vomiting.
Step 3: By allowing the spouse to pack an overnight bag calmly, the patient reduces environmental stress, promoting a stable resting state while awaiting transport.
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A - Hospitalization required.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Handover to tertiary hospital emergency department.

